updated and repaired.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.160
So a lot of providers might
update their master version

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.980
automatically, but then
leave node management to you.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.410
We kind of believe that
the whole thing needs

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.990
to work together, and so
we actually automatically

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.950
can update and repair your node.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.950
So if you go from
1.12 to 1.13, we

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.500
can actually-- as your
master version gets updated,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.118
we can update your node
version for you automatically.

00:03:09.118 --> 00:03:11.160
And if something goes
wrong with the node-- let's

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.545
say the Docker daemon dies,
the kubelet, something

00:03:13.545 --> 00:03:15.920
goes wrong with it, we can do
that-- we can automatically

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.050
detect that with the health
check, delete that node,

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.508
and spin up a new
one automatically

00:03:19.508 --> 00:03:20.328
to take its place.

00:03:20.328 --> 00:03:21.870
Again, you don't
have to do anything,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.162
you don't have to worry about
the actual infrastructure

00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:24.867
anymore.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:32.200
So another really cool feature
of GCE is node customization.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.690
So you can make something
called custom VMs with a custom

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.830
amount of CPU, memory, throw
on the local SSDs, GPUs,

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.350
things like that, and you get
all that same capabilities

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.550
in GKE.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.110
What this means is
you're not limited

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:46.840
to certain types of machines
that can run on GKE.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.830
Any GCE VM can also run on GKE.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.300
And this gives you a
lot more flexibility.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.720
If you need a beefy node
to do some sort of workload

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.970
and you need a really small
node to do something else,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.400
you can have that mix and
match of capabilities.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.930
If you need GPUs like a P100
or V100 or whatever, easier

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.570
to add onto your GKE cluster.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:10.930
And speaking of
picking the right size,

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.500
GCE actually has a machine
learning algorithm built into--

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.980
do something called cluster node
right sizing recommendations.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.860
So here we can actually look
at your utilization of your VMs

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.050
and say, hey, you're
overutilizing your VMs.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.630
You should probably pump
up the RAM a little bit

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.210
so you can get some more
smoother performance,

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:31.000
or this machine is way too
big, you're wasting money.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.660
Downsize it, save a bunch
of money on your bill.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.860
And again, because
it's built on GCE,

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.660
all these features
pipe directly into GKE.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.070
Now one of my favorite, favorite
things is preemptible nodes.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.260
And I don't think people use
these nearly enough to take

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.350
advantage of them.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.220
So I actually did a three-month
data collection experiment

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.320
where I ran an app that
was constantly paying

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.447
and I wrote a bunch of data and
I did a bunch of data analysis,

00:04:55.447 --> 00:04:57.030
and I found out that
you can basically

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.180
run three times your
capacity for the same cost

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.452
and get almost no
detectable baseline

00:05:03.452 --> 00:05:04.410
like difference, right?

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.840
So if you had one
node for $10 and you

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.470
had three nodes now for $10,
yes, those preemptible nodes

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.240
can die, they get
preempted every 24 hours,

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:17.220
they might get shut down, but
it happens at such an irregular

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.810
basis and it happens--
and Kubernetes

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.768
is so resilient, I'm
spinning up the containers

00:05:20.768 --> 00:05:22.230
to take its place,
that if you're

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.730
running stateless
workloads and you're not

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.530
using preemptible VMs,
you are definitely

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.270
leaving money on the table.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:32.035
I wrote a whole blog post about
this where I go into detail.

00:05:32.035 --> 00:05:34.465
Ooh, animations.

00:05:34.465 --> 00:05:36.340
So of course, I talked
about how you can kind

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:40.310
mix and match those different
types of VMs together.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.748
And so the way we do that in GKE
is something called node pools.

00:05:43.748 --> 00:05:45.790
And so again, something
that I don't think people

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:48.700
take advantage of, most people
make a Kubernetes cluster

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.290
and they have one type of node.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:52.390
You can actually take
different types of nodes,

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:53.910
give them different
labels and tags,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.660
and then run different
workloads on those.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.390
So your database can
go on a more CPU-heavy,

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.490
memory-heavy node;
your stateless web app

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.130
can go on the smaller
nodes that are cheaper;

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.583
your machine learning workloads
can go on your GPU nodes;

00:06:05.583 --> 00:06:07.750
and you can have them all
in the same cluster, which

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:10.083
makes networking and all that
kind of stuff a lot easier

00:06:10.083 --> 00:06:11.790
to setup.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:15.560
And another big feature that
we rolled out about last year

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:16.700
is regional clusters.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.420
So by default, clusters are
created in a single zone,

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:20.270
right?

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.110
The problem with that
is-- there's twofold.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.740
One, if that zone goes down,
well then you're out of luck.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.560
And two, during
a master upgrade,

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:29.882
the cluster might still
work, but the API server

00:06:29.882 --> 00:06:30.590
goes down, right?

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.038
So you can't give it updates
and things like that.

00:06:33.038 --> 00:06:34.580
But the regional
cluster, we actually

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:37.910
spread those nodes over
three different zones

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.720
in the same region.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.635
And so this gives you one,
higher availability, right?

00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:44.260
If a zone goes down,
you're still good,

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:46.880
you still have these two other
zones that are up and running.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.585
And two, higher master
availability because you

00:06:49.585 --> 00:06:51.110
have highly available masters.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:55.040
Because we have three different
masters, one in each zone.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.380
When we do an upgrade, we
actually do it one at a time

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.290
so we never lose access to
your Kubernetes API server.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.320
Really cool stuff.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.120
And if you're using
regional clusters,

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.040
you can use regional
persistent disks.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:08.930
Now these are
really, really cool.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:10.690
So basically it
creates two disks,

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:12.790
and one is a clone of the other.

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:14.680
And any write that's
sent to the first disk

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:16.450
is just forked at
our network level

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.160
to both disks the same time.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.070
So any writes that go in are
actually written to both disks

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:24.910
simultaneously with no
performance downgrade, right?

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.640
And so what happens
if zone A goes down,

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.520
zone B becomes hot and
starts serving data,

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:33.790
and there's no real disruption
to the end application.

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:36.000
This is actually how
Cloud SQL for Postgres

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:36.995
runs under the covers.

00:07:36.995 --> 00:07:38.620
It uses these regional
persistent disks

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:40.060
to provide high availability.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.210
And because GKE has native
built-in connections

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:47.750
to all of the stuff, you
can use it as well in GKE.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.850
Now another important
thing is autoscaling.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.490
And so everyone can do
autoscaling based on CPU.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:56.960
Pretty trivial, but with
Stackdriver and with GKE,

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.240
you can autoscale
on custom metrics.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:02.240
So anything your app needs,
like number of incoming requests

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.220
or a number of
open file handlers,

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.990
whatever you need
to scale your app,

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:09.170
you can send that to Stackdriver
and scale your GKE nodes

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:11.660
based on those metrics.

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:14.480
And speaking of
autoscaling, GKE provides

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:16.813
something called
vertical pod autoscaling.

00:08:16.813 --> 00:08:18.980
Now how many people are
familiar with horizontal pod

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.450
autoscaling?

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:21.097
Most people.

00:08:21.097 --> 00:08:23.430
So horizontal pod autoscaling
is pretty straightforward.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:25.970
You run out of CPU, you
spin up another pod,

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.490
you spin up another pod,
it spins out horizontally.

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:30.860
But vertical pod
autoscaling can actually

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:34.039
set your memory
requests per container

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:36.020
and actually make it
them go bigger or smaller

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.200
depending on the workload.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.783
We actually again
use machine learning

00:08:38.783 --> 00:08:40.919
to figure out how much
memory your container needs.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:43.044
So if it needs more, we'll
actually make-- redeploy

00:08:43.044 --> 00:08:45.680
those pods with more requests,
and if they need less,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:47.050
we'll spin them down with less.

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:49.070
So we're actually
spinning the pod requests

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:51.540
up and down for
you automatically.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.710
So we can do both vertical pod
autoscaling, horizontal pod

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:57.410
autoscaling, as well
as cluster autoscaling.

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.330
So it's like multiple dimensions
of autoscaling all at once,

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:04.540
and we optimize that all for
you, which is really cool.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:06.910
Ooh, look at that.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:10.600
And of course today we
announce Anthos, GKE On-Prem.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:14.080
So we can do all this really
cool GKE stuff or most of it

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.860
not only on our cloud, but
on-prem and in other providers

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:18.560
as well.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:20.230
So that's really
cool, and there's

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:23.720
other talks that can go
more into detail on that.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.300
So I talked about
the infrastructure,

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:27.092
and now I want to really
get into the bread

00:09:27.092 --> 00:09:29.970
and butter of where I think
GCP shines, especially

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:31.180
in terms of GKE.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:34.110
So when you go from a monolith
or a VM-based architecture,

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:36.330
a lot of the networking
assumptions you make change

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:38.830
dramatically when you moved to
containers and microservices,

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:39.580
right?

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:41.122
The networking gets
way more complex,

00:09:41.122 --> 00:09:42.872
and if you're not built
from the ground up

00:09:42.872 --> 00:09:45.570
to support that kind of stuff,
things can fall apart, right?

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.290
The old paradigms
kind of fall apart.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:50.220
So let's talk about
networking first.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.370
So normally you have your
nodes and you have pods

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:54.570
on those nodes.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:57.090
And so these nodes are VMs,
and they have IP addresses

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:59.250
on the VPC, right?

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:01.680
But those pods, the
IP addresses that they

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.960
have are virtual
IPs that are only

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:05.130
routable within the cluster.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:11.820
So the problem here is that when
you want to send a request out,

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:15.910
those IP addresses
get masked with NAT,

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:18.710
and so the node IP address
goes out, not the actual pods.

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:20.702
So you don't really know
which pod on that node

00:10:20.702 --> 00:10:21.660
is making that request.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:24.360
And the same thing coming in,
you don't-- it's really hard

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.270
to route traffic to a pod on
the node because you have to go

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:29.200
through the node first.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:33.990
But with IP aliasing
or VPC native pod IPs,

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:36.900
those pods actually get
real GCP IP addresses

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.840
that are natively
routable on the VPC.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:41.060
So what does this let you do?

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:42.435
It lets you do
really cool things

00:10:42.435 --> 00:10:44.380
like container-native
load balancing.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.190
So in the old world, you
spin up a load balancer.

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:49.650
That load balancer has no
idea what's running inside

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:50.700
of those VMs, right?

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.050
It just knows-- send
traffic to that pod--

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:57.780
sorry, to that VM on this
port, and then IP tables inside

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:01.300
of that node has to do another
routing to the actual pod,

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:01.800
right?

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.150
And that pod might not even
be running on the same VM.

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:05.670
We call it a double-hop problem.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:08.672
So it might go to
one virtual machine

00:11:08.672 --> 00:11:10.630
and then get routed to
another virtual machine,

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:12.030
and then go to the pod, right?

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.680
Lot of hops, a lot
of latency that's

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:16.270
added to your infrastructure.

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:18.250
With container-native
load balancing,

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:21.812
our load balancers actually
know the physical IP addresses--

00:11:21.812 --> 00:11:24.020
well, not physical, everything's
virtual these days--

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:26.830
but the actual IP
address of the pod itself

00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:29.020
and can route traffic
directly into that pod

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:31.660
without going through IP tables,
without going through NATing,

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:32.930
without any of that stuff.

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:35.390
So you get a lot less
latency, and more importantly,

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:36.575
you get more transparency.

00:11:36.575 --> 00:11:38.200
You don't have to go
through your logs.

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:40.600
If you go through your VPC
flow logs and like, OK, well

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.600
it went from this VM to that
VM and then to that container.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:46.510
Instead it's just from that
load balancer, that container,

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:47.620
straight line, right?

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:53.403
Much easier to reason with
and debug and analyze.

00:11:53.403 --> 00:11:54.820
And speaking of
load balancing, we

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:56.960
have internal load
balancing as well.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:00.010
And so this, again, it's
straight built into Kubernetes,

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:02.590
so we can provision an internal
load balancer the same way

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:04.315
as an external load balancer.

00:12:04.315 --> 00:12:08.170
And so this lets you expose
your Kubernetes services

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.840
to non-Kubernetes
services inside GKE--

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:12.160
inside Google Cloud, right?

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:15.850
So if you have a service running
on VMs, a traditional kind

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:18.850
of thing that you haven't
migrated to GKE yet,

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:21.238
you can just expose
your GKE service,

00:12:21.238 --> 00:12:22.780
and it gives you an
IP address that's

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:24.822
routable inside of the VPC.

00:12:24.822 --> 00:12:26.530
And the really nice
thing is because this

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:28.990
is done with an SDN,
there's no overhead,

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:30.490
and everything's
just done-- there's

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:31.657
no box sitting there, right?

00:12:31.657 --> 00:12:34.977
We just know where
to send the traffic.

00:12:34.977 --> 00:12:36.060
And the other way around--

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:36.602
Cloud NAT.

00:12:36.602 --> 00:12:38.310
So if you have a
third-party service that

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:42.213
requires a whitelist
of IPs, well, it's

00:12:42.213 --> 00:12:43.380
kind of hard to do with GKE.

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.660
Those nodes come and
go, the IP addresses

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.430
change, you might
spin up your cluster

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:49.080
to make it bigger
or smaller, so how

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:51.300
do you give them a
whitelist of IPs?

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.600
Well with Cloud NAT, you can
actually create an IP address,

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.752
and all the traffic gets
routed through that IP address.

00:12:57.752 --> 00:13:00.210
So it looks like all the traffic
is coming from a single IP

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:02.740
address, but it's scalable
because again, we're

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:04.800
doing SDN magic under
the covers to make

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.170
it look like the traffic
is coming from this IP.

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.342
So you just give that IP
to your third party person,

00:13:09.342 --> 00:13:11.050
they whitelist it,
and you're good to go.

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:13.290
You don't have to worry
about managing a list of IPs

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.030
and things like that.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:17.880
And then we have
multi-cluster ingress.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:18.910
So this is really cool.

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:20.970
We have a bunch of
clusters around the world.

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:24.270
You can spin up a single load
balancer with a single IP

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:27.570
address, and it can route
traffic to all of your clusters

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:28.470
automatically, right?

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:30.480
So we'll actually find
the closest cluster

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:33.810
to the user using anycast,
and we can route their traffic

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:35.670
to that cluster without
you having to do

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.350
DNS or anything complicated.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.350
And I actually have a talk a few
years ago at Next where I spun

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:43.320
up a multi-cluster ingress
using [? Ku ?] [? Federation, ?]

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:44.860
which is quite a while ago.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:48.040
So if you want to check
that out, check it out.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:51.070
And then we have
deep integration

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:54.490
with other networking products
like CDN, IAP, and Cloud Armor.

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:58.210
So Cloud CDN, as the
name implies, is our CDN.

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:00.440
Really powerful stuff.

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:05.230
IAP, Identity-Aware Proxy, lets
you throw this little proxy

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:08.220
in front of our load balancer
that-- we call it BeyondCorp,

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:09.190
you can read the paper.

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:12.390
Basically like you don't
have to build security--

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:15.490
if your application requires
something like a VPN

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:16.490
to connect to it, right?

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:18.095
If you're using
VPN for security,

00:14:18.095 --> 00:14:20.470
there's an alternative where
you don't have to use a VPN.

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:22.840
Instead, you can use
OAuth to authenticate you

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:25.755
as an end user, and then
log in to that service.

00:14:25.755 --> 00:14:27.130
So for your
application, it still

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:29.830
looks like it's in the
VPN, but this proxy kind of

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:32.620
lets you do that without
VPNs, which is really nice.

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:35.160
And then Cloud Armor, which
is our anti-DDoS protection.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:37.000
And they're not
just built add-ons.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:39.310
These are-- you can actually
configure all these things

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:43.670
with Kubernetes-native
objects, which is really cool.

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:46.180
All right, so security.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:48.680
Security is really important,
especially in Kubernetes land.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:52.490
How many people saw the Tesla
like cryptomining thing?

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:52.990
Yeah.

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:55.180
So their cluster got
hacked and people started

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:56.920
running crypto stuff on it.

00:14:56.920 --> 00:14:58.660
And so what I'm going
to talk about now

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:01.600
is kind of layers of security
that we add to make it more

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.120
harder for an attacker to
get into your systems, right?

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:05.800
So the first thing
is private clusters

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:07.870
and master authorized networks.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:10.790
Private clusters, it's
literally what the name implies.

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:12.880
The cluster doesn't
have public IPs,

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:16.230
therefore it's only
internal, right?

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.190
And again, master
authorized networks,

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:20.233
only certain people can
log into the master,

00:15:20.233 --> 00:15:22.400
not some random person on
the internet cannot, like,

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:24.010
SSH into your master, right?

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:25.942
So again, just another
layer of protection.

00:15:25.942 --> 00:15:27.400
So if they break
into your network,

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.442
then yeah, they still
could log into your master,

00:15:29.442 --> 00:15:31.310
so how do we get around that?

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:33.520
So we have something
called network policy.

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:36.758
Network policy in
GKE, we automatically

00:15:36.758 --> 00:15:38.800
install something called
Calico into your cluster

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:42.760
to make sure that when you
set these network policies,

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:43.505
they're enforced.

00:15:43.505 --> 00:15:45.130
And so these are
really powerful stuff.

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:48.310
You can say, this namespace can
talk to this namespace, but not

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:49.490
the other way around.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:51.940
Or these pods can
talk to these pods,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:54.352
but they can't talk
to any other pods.

00:15:54.352 --> 00:15:56.560
And so my co-worker Ahmet
has a really good blog post

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:59.680
and a GitHub repo that goes
through all these namespace--

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:01.930
sorry, these network
policies and how they work.

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:02.850
Highly recommend
checking it out,

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:04.480
it's where I stole
this picture from.

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:06.670
But I guess you get another
layer of control, right?

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:10.660
So someone is able to
break into the app-web,

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.970
they can talk to any
containers but the containers

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:14.093
can't talk back, right?

00:16:14.093 --> 00:16:16.510
So you get a little bit more
control over what's going on.

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:20.018
Kubernetes secrets.

00:16:20.018 --> 00:16:21.810
So for the longest
time, Kubernetes secrets

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:23.725
were not encrypted.

00:16:23.725 --> 00:16:26.100
In more recent versions of
Kubernetes they are encrypted,

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:30.030
and with GKE, you can actually
encrypt them with Cloud KMS,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:31.822
and so you can manage
those encryption keys

00:16:31.822 --> 00:16:33.197
and rotate them
and do everything

00:16:33.197 --> 00:16:35.790
that you can with Cloud KMS,
now for your Kubernetes-native

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:36.570
secrets.

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:38.430
Again, just making it
easier to use, right?

00:16:42.532 --> 00:16:43.990
And then binary
authorization, this

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:45.410
is a really cool new feature.

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:47.910
Basically, let's say someone
does break into your Kubernetes

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:51.600
cluster, and now they want
to run cryptomine.latest

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:52.770
or something like that.

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:55.000
Well, they can't, because
it's not authorized.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:59.070
So we can actually enforce
signed containers--

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:01.020
so only containers
that are signed by you

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:03.330
can actually even run
on your cluster, right?

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:06.150
So again, rogue developer
who wants to run something

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:08.925
can't do it, it
has to be signed.

00:17:08.925 --> 00:17:10.050
So this is really powerful.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.630
Again, locking down another
layer of security that we

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:15.490
give you in GKE.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:17.060
Now how many people
know about this?

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:19.559
Container registry
vulnerability scanning?

00:17:19.559 --> 00:17:20.829
A few people.

00:17:20.829 --> 00:17:22.460
Yeah, so this is really cool.

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:25.190
If you're using
Alpine or Ubuntu,

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:28.520
it actually automatically scans
your containers for known CVEs

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:30.170
and it'll surface those for you.

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:32.540
So you can see, I uploaded
some really old containers

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:35.108
and have tons of
issues, as expected.

00:17:35.108 --> 00:17:36.650
And you can go
through and see what's

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:38.540
important to you, what
you should update,

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:39.470
and things like that.

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:41.930
Usually if you're building
containers fairly regularly,

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:43.840
they should be
fairly up to date,

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:45.720
but this is a good
way to see like, oh, I

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:47.660
have an old container with
a bunch of vulnerabilities,

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:49.760
let me update it, or hey,
this new vulnerability

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.970
came out, let me make sure that
I'm not being affected, right?

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:57.190
So really cool stuff, and
again, built straight into GCR.

00:17:57.190 --> 00:17:59.465
So the last section,
monitoring and tools.

00:17:59.465 --> 00:18:01.750
So here are things again I
think people forget about,

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:04.080
or they're just kind
of, heh, it's there.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:04.790
I don't remember.

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:06.457
But when you don't
have it, you're like,

00:18:06.457 --> 00:18:08.190
I really wish I did.

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:12.020
So one is Stackdriver
Kubernetes Monitoring.

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:15.770
There are quite a few tools
nowadays to monitor Kubernetes.

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:19.370
Stackdriver has been updated to
monitor not just the VMs that

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:21.958
are running your
cluster, but actually

00:18:21.958 --> 00:18:24.250
your pods and your services
that are internally, right?

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:26.040
So here we can see that--
we can't see anything

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:27.832
because that's like a
super blurry picture,

00:18:27.832 --> 00:18:30.110
but if you could see that
picture, you would actually

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:33.530
see the nodes, the pods in those
nodes, how much memory they

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:37.535
have, they're healthy, all the
custom metrics that you set all

00:18:37.535 --> 00:18:40.860
on one pane of glass,
which is really nice.

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:42.830
CI/CD is necessary.

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:45.000
Cloud Build is one
way of doing that.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.780
And I think Cloud Build is a
really easy lightweight CI/CD

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:50.880
tool that you can
use, especially for--

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:52.890
if you don't have
anything too complicated,

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:56.720
you just put your steps
in, it runs those commands,

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:58.500
and it can build your
container for you.

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:00.560
And you can do more
complicated stuff

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:03.077
like actually deploy a
Kubernetes, run health checks,

00:19:03.077 --> 00:19:04.160
do all this kind of stuff.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:07.190
It's a very, very
simplistic product,

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:08.900
but it really has
a lot of power.

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:10.610
And because it's
completely serverless,

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:13.220
you don't have to worry about
provisioning a CI/CD like Build

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:14.570
pipeline or anything like that.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:17.510
You literally just run a job
and it just runs it for you,

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:19.670
and you get tons of
free Build minutes

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:22.520
per day, which is really cool.

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:23.110
Now this one.

00:19:23.110 --> 00:19:25.930
It's something that most people,
again, don't really think

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:30.130
is a differentiator, but the GKE
Dashboard is really powerful.

00:19:30.130 --> 00:19:32.380
You can see your workloads,
you can see your services,

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:34.797
you can drill really
deep into these things.

00:19:34.797 --> 00:19:35.880
There's a really nice GUI.

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:37.720
You can edit them
right from there.

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:40.653
You can create new deployments,
you can look at the status,

00:19:40.653 --> 00:19:42.070
you can do all
this kind of stuff,

00:19:42.070 --> 00:19:44.037
again, without writing
a single line of code

00:19:44.037 --> 00:19:45.745
or going into single
command line, right?

00:19:45.745 --> 00:19:49.520
Right from that
pantheon dashboard

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:54.180
you can see everything that
you need, which is really nice.

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.580
The Service Broker.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:20:00.530
This one is really useful if you
want your Kubernetes operators

00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:03.230
to be able to provision
other GCP services

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:05.630
or, for that matter,
open-source services or even

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:08.960
other cloud services directly
from GKE objects, right?

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:11.762
So you can say, hey, spin up
a Cloud Firestore database,

00:20:11.762 --> 00:20:13.220
and it'll just spin
that up for you

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:16.550
and give you those credentials
back as native GKE secrets.

00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:20.750
Really, really powerful, and it
gives you a better integration

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:22.580
into the rest of
both Google Cloud

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:25.567
and open-source ecosystem.

00:20:25.567 --> 00:20:27.900
And finally, I'm going to
talk about namespace and label

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:29.110
resource usage.

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:31.570
So how many people
here have a GKE cluster

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:33.270
that is shared between teams?

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:34.370
A few people.

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:36.120
So a really cool thing
is you can actually

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:40.330
export usage based on
namespaces and based on labels.

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:42.270
So let's say that you
have namespace X that's

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:43.167
used by team 1.

00:20:43.167 --> 00:20:45.000
You can actually see
how much of the cluster

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:47.880
that they use
versus namespace C.

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:49.300
And the same for your apps.

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:51.000
So if you have two
apps labeled app A

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:52.500
and they're in
different namespaces,

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:56.370
you can, again, get that
export of the resource usage

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:59.140
for those apps
outside of the place.

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:00.810
So really cool stuff,
especially if you

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:02.160
have multiple teams
using the same cluster

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:04.452
and you want to break that
down for analytics purposes.

00:21:06.983 --> 00:21:09.400
So we have to add-ons that I
want to talk about, because I

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:10.650
think they're really powerful.

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:13.075
So one is the GKE Istio add-on.

00:21:13.075 --> 00:21:15.200
You click one button when
you're creating a cluster

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.500
and you get Istio installed
into your cluster for you

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:18.610
automatically.

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:19.390
Really cool stuff.

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:20.890
If you don't know
what Istio is, I'm

00:21:20.890 --> 00:21:22.900
sure there's some
talks around here

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:24.700
that you could
probably wander into,

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:28.000
but long story short, it's
called a service mesh,

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:29.648
and it really adds
a lot of value

00:21:29.648 --> 00:21:31.690
to you if you're doing
microservices architecture

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:33.190
on GKE.

00:21:33.190 --> 00:21:35.590
It adds a lot of value for
you automatically without you

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:38.800
having to do too much work.

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:41.630
If you don't what Istio is, go
look it up, it's pretty cool.

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:44.300
And then we have Cloud
Run on GKE as well.

00:21:44.300 --> 00:21:47.920
And so this built on
top of Istio and Knative

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:50.890
to be able to run serverless
workloads on top of GKE.

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:53.740
So it's the same Cloud Run APIs
that you can run serverlessly

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:55.870
on GCP.

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:58.240
You can actually run it
inside of a Kubernetes cluster

00:21:58.240 --> 00:21:59.180
as well.

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:01.083
So you get the same
developer experience end

00:22:01.083 --> 00:22:02.500
to end no matter
if you're running

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:08.000
on our serverless platform or
on our Kubernetes platform.

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:10.400
But don't take my word for it.

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:12.830
With me, I have
Brad from Symphony.

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:14.230
Symphony Communications.

00:22:14.230 --> 00:22:15.813
He's a VP of
Architecture here, and I

00:22:15.813 --> 00:22:17.230
want to give him
the floor to kind

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:19.170
of talk about his
GKE experience,

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:20.980
and here you go, yeah.

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:22.640
BRAD DIETRICH: Thanks, Sandeep.

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:24.860
And so as many of
you, we kind of

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:27.980
embarked on the Kubernetes
path a while ago

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:30.710
looking at the ability to
containerize an application.

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:32.660
The application,
of course, has been

00:22:32.660 --> 00:22:34.940
around for longer than
what we actually wanted

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:36.350
to do with containerization.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:39.290
We were doing a lot of the heavy
lifting that Sandeep described.

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:41.840
We were running
virtual machines,

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:43.850
we were maintaining
those virtual machines,

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:47.690
operating systems up, and trying
to keep a secure platform.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:49.310
What Symphony
actually is is it's

00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:52.110
a secure team
collaboration platform,

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:55.670
but it is really focused on
financial services customers

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:57.840
as our primary business segment.

00:22:57.840 --> 00:22:59.840
And if you've ever worked
with those customers,

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:02.640
you know that they are
extremely sensitive to security.

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:06.710
So security was always kind at
the core of everything we do.

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:10.490
And we've worked for a couple
of years now with the GKE team

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:13.070
specifically around crafting
the security messages

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:14.877
that you heard from Sandeep.

00:23:14.877 --> 00:23:16.460
Working with product
managers, telling

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:20.420
them what was important for
us and our customer base

00:23:20.420 --> 00:23:22.970
so that it becomes
more accessible

00:23:22.970 --> 00:23:24.560
across the entire industry.

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:27.260
Not just something that
you can actually focus on

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:29.360
from an internet-based
application, but actually

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:32.240
something that individual banks
and large financial services

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:34.750
institutions would
start to trust as well.

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:36.500
And that's really,
again, what Symphony is

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:38.900
and where we see a
lot of value and a lot

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:41.190
of continued innovation.

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:43.340
And in trying to drive
that security, things

00:23:43.340 --> 00:23:48.440
like the private GKE clusters,
the ability to actually know

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:51.770
that banks still think about
how we're going to actually get

00:23:51.770 --> 00:23:54.830
into our networks, make sure
that everything is signed,

00:23:54.830 --> 00:23:56.600
signed images are
going to be very

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:58.730
important from our
perspective as well.

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:00.620
So that we can actually
have high confidence

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:03.050
that the cluster is only
running what we want it to run,

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:05.750
and the individual teams
within both our organization

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:07.790
and our customers'
organizations have

00:24:07.790 --> 00:24:10.730
good visibility and good
auditability around what that's

00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:13.340
running and where those
individual pieces are

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:14.980
able to talk to each other.

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:15.855
SANDEEP DINESH: Cool.

00:24:15.855 --> 00:24:17.920
So Brad, I have some
canned questions for you.

00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:18.860
BRAD DIETRICH: Sure.

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.030
SANDEEP DINESH: And then
we'll open up the floor

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:21.380
to questions from the audience.

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:23.180
Again, remember the
Dory is available

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:25.070
if you want to just
type it in if you don't

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:26.690
want to come up to the mic.

00:24:26.690 --> 00:24:29.900
So when you chose
GKE as your platform,

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:32.600
what was like one of the
biggest challenges moving from--

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:34.460
again, you said like a
VM-based architecture

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:36.170
to that container-based
architecture?

00:24:36.170 --> 00:24:37.003
BRAD DIETRICH: Sure.

00:24:37.003 --> 00:24:40.220
So we actually are dual-hosted.

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:43.370
We've been hosted in AWS
and GCP for a long time.

00:24:43.370 --> 00:24:46.460
We had been on both
in the VM world,

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:51.190
but we treated our VMs very
much like you would consider

00:24:51.190 --> 00:24:52.190
an internal data center.

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:53.732
And in fact, part
of our architecture

00:24:53.732 --> 00:24:56.325
does include our customers'
data centers as well, that's

00:24:56.325 --> 00:24:58.700
one of the reasons that we
can actually have the security

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:00.230
model that we have.

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:01.820
So we've always
been looking at ways

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:04.070
to optimize the efficiencies
around how do we

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:08.420
administer AWS, GCP, and
our customers' data centers.

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:11.960
And that's really where
Kubernetes and containerization

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:13.030
shines.

00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:15.530
It gives us the ability to
package our application in ways

00:25:15.530 --> 00:25:18.260
that we can deliver to
our customers on-premise.

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:21.510
They do it in GKE
On-Prem in some cases,

00:25:21.510 --> 00:25:24.980
but they do it in Cloud
Foundry or other things that

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:27.920
are container-based
orchestration systems.

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:30.710
We build our containers
for our cloud system

00:25:30.710 --> 00:25:33.760
to really kind of evolve
the platform there.

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:35.250
And it was some heavy lifting.

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.952
I mean, many of you have
probably been through it.

00:25:37.952 --> 00:25:40.160
The assumptions around VM,
some of the things Sandeep

00:25:40.160 --> 00:25:42.450
already mentioned, they
have to be rethought,

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:44.570
and the architecture
has to be evolved.

00:25:44.570 --> 00:25:47.450
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so
speaking of re-architecting, so

00:25:47.450 --> 00:25:50.160
has Kubernetes and
GKE kind of forced

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:51.680
you to re-architect
or reconsider

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:54.077
some design decisions that
you've made in the past?

00:25:54.077 --> 00:25:55.910
BRAD DIETRICH: It is,
but I mean, part of it

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:57.410
is-- you mentioned
monolith earlier,

00:25:57.410 --> 00:26:01.010
and our original application,
like most as early startups

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:03.512
get started, tends to be
a little bit monolithic.

00:26:03.512 --> 00:26:05.720
And so that's really what
we're looking at right now,

00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:07.790
is how do we break the
monolith in such a way

00:26:07.790 --> 00:26:10.850
to gain resiliency, gain scale
with the right layers that

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:11.870
make sense.

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:17.810
Allow teams to operate-- because
we have 153 engineers depending

00:26:17.810 --> 00:26:20.660
on how you count the
external help that we have--

00:26:20.660 --> 00:26:22.610
that are driving in parallel.

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:24.520
And as you guys
know, trying to get

00:26:24.520 --> 00:26:27.020
everybody aligned and everybody
headed in the same direction

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:29.240
is really difficult,
and microservices can

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:30.630
make a big difference there.

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:34.160
We're still breaking apart
the monolith to some extent,

00:26:34.160 --> 00:26:36.410
but that's the evolution
path that many of you are on,

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:37.340
and that's where
we're on as well.

00:26:37.340 --> 00:26:39.632
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so what
would you say the biggest

00:26:39.632 --> 00:26:41.390
challenge in breaking--
or decomposing,

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.580
let's say that, it's a
nice word-- in decomposing

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:44.510
your monolith, right?

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:47.690
So it's a really long
journey, as you mentioned.

00:26:47.690 --> 00:26:51.530
What's kind of been the hardest
part, and then how has GKE

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:52.950
kind of helped you do that?

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:53.300
BRAD DIETRICH: Sure.

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:53.800
Yeah.

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:56.700
So I mean, the nice thing
about GKE is the ease of access

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:58.850
that Sandeep already
mentioned-- spin up

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:01.160
a cluster with one command.

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:05.330
We've had kind of our engineers
that we've been training--

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:08.240
Google actually offers a bunch
of training support, too.

00:27:08.240 --> 00:27:11.960
Our TMs have come in and brought
in individuals that show us

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:14.390
and our teams how to use
Kubernetes the right way,

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:17.780
the best practices way so we can
actually evolve the thinking.

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:21.290
The hardest part in my
opinion of how to do this

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:23.150
is rarely the technical part.

00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:24.770
The technical part,
a couple people

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:27.110
can sit around a whiteboard
and break up the thing

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:29.045
and decide the right
way to break it up,

00:27:29.045 --> 00:27:30.920
the right contracts to
make sure the services

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:33.050
talk to each other.

00:27:33.050 --> 00:27:35.450
But if the engineering
team-- if the talent that you

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:38.240
have don't understand
the tools well enough

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.190
or don't understand the
methodologies you're

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:42.780
trying to achieve, that's where
you can get a lot of advantage

00:27:42.780 --> 00:27:44.918
just by the
accessibility of GKE.

00:27:44.918 --> 00:27:46.460
There's no reason
that we should have

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:49.250
to spin our own Kubernetes
cluster to learn Kubernetes

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:50.478
with GKE.

00:27:50.478 --> 00:27:51.270
SANDEEP DINESH: OK.

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:53.390
And so final question.

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:56.250
What are-- and this is
two questions in one.

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:59.840
What's the most exciting
thing about GKE for you?

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:00.830
Or the future of GKE.

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:01.730
Like what's the
most exciting thing

00:28:01.730 --> 00:28:04.520
that you see on the roadmap
that you're excited about maybe

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:06.010
that's public?

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:10.583
And then more personally, what
do you guys are building inside

00:28:10.583 --> 00:28:12.500
of Symphony that you
really want to talk about

00:28:12.500 --> 00:28:13.750
and are super excited about?

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:15.500
BRAD DIETRICH: Well,
I can't talk too much

00:28:15.500 --> 00:28:17.450
about the latter one.

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:18.040
Again, we--

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:19.040
SANDEEP DINESH: I tried.

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:20.915
BRAD DIETRICH: We are
doing a bunch of things

00:28:20.915 --> 00:28:22.580
around workflow automation.

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:25.790
You see these things happening,
you see autonomous agents

00:28:25.790 --> 00:28:28.430
happening in the industry.

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:31.520
We are a tool very similar
to Slack or Microsoft Teams,

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:33.530
although we're focused
much more on the security

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:35.720
and the compliance aspects.

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:37.220
But all those same
things that you

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:41.120
see happening on those tools,
even things like chat ops

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:43.680
that you guys are probably
already doing in your teams,

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:46.010
are just as relevant
for our customers

00:28:46.010 --> 00:28:48.062
and our financial
services industry.

00:28:48.062 --> 00:28:49.520
And so we really
see a lot of value

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:52.790
about bringing
containerization to those tools

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:55.680
that our customers
are building as well.

00:28:55.680 --> 00:28:56.780
And one of the pieces--

00:28:56.780 --> 00:28:58.910
you mentioned GKE On-Prem.

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:01.250
One of the pieces that
is still difficult for us

00:29:01.250 --> 00:29:03.445
and our customers is
that on-prem piece.

00:29:03.445 --> 00:29:05.820
We'll never get rid of it.
it's part of our architecture,

00:29:05.820 --> 00:29:08.190
it's a key part of
our architecture.

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:12.200
We also won't be administering
our own on-prem clusters

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:14.280
because they're our
customers' data centers.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:17.600
But they may opt to use
more and more of GKE On-Prem

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:22.160
to really kind of aid themselves
in making that an easier

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:23.600
tool for them to use.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:25.590
But our customers are
also, like I said,

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:26.600
very security conscious.

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:29.390
And so we have to
understand that some

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:33.470
of the kind of cloud-managed
on-prem infrastructure for them

00:29:33.470 --> 00:29:36.380
is something that they have
to kind of work through

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:38.958
and understand the
security implications of.

00:29:38.958 --> 00:29:41.000
They're really uncomfortable
with things reaching

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:42.950
from the cloud back in.

00:29:42.950 --> 00:29:48.820
They have very constricted
firewalls and border security

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:50.360
in a lot of their data centers.

00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:54.260
So it's something to
consider, and as GKE evolves,

00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:56.360
certainly there's
kind of an air gap

00:29:56.360 --> 00:30:00.170
notion of potentially how
we might view and administer

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:02.450
a cluster without actually
having direct access to it.

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:03.325
SANDEEP DINESH: Cool.

00:30:03.325 --> 00:30:06.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

